# Tooling parity: Claude Code and Cursor

Ported from `.claude/rules/tooling-parity.md` — keep both in sync by hand if either changes (yes,
this rule applies to itself).

This repo has contributors on both Claude Code and Cursor. Keep the two tools' guardrails,
subagents, and skills at equal functionality — never let one tool's users get access to a
guardrail, checklist, or piece of context that the other tool's users don't.

## Why this matters more than usual "keep docs in sync" advice

Most of what's mirrored here isn't documentation — it's the access-control, data-privacy, and
parallel-work guardrails from `docs/requirements/project-requirements.md` Sections 2, 3, and 7,
which are safety-critical, not stylistic. A guardrail that only reaches one tool's users is a gap
the other tool's contributor won't know exists, on a project where access control is explicitly
the primary graded quality attribute.

## The rule

- Any new or edited `.cursor/rules/*.mdc` needs a matching `.claude/rules/*.md`, and vice versa.
  Always-on Cursor rules (`alwaysApply: true`, empty `globs`) correspond to guardrails Claude Code
  loads via root `CLAUDE.md`; glob-scoped Cursor rules correspond to path-scoped
  `.claude/rules/*.md` or `.claude/agents/*.md` content.
- `.claude/agents/*.md` subagents (isolated tools/context) don't have a literal Cursor equivalent
  — port them as plain instructions/checklists in a glob-scoped `.mdc` instead, scoped to
  whatever path the subagent was originally scoped to.
- `.cursor/rules/00-project-overview.mdc` mirrors root `CLAUDE.md` — Cursor doesn't read
  `CLAUDE.md` directly, so any change there needs the mirror updated too.
- BMAD skills (`.claude/skills/bmad-*`) are already mirrored to `.agents/skills/bmad-*` by the
  BMAD installer itself (`cursor` → `.agents/skills` per `bmad-method install --list-tools`) —
  installer-managed, not hand-maintained.
- When auditing parity, compare **topic coverage**, not byte-for-byte content — the two formats
  differ by design (frontmatter, auto-attach model).
- Per-area rules that live under a service's own `services/<name>/.claude/rules/*.md` (e.g. the
  `nest-*.md` rules shared by the six NestJS backend services, or `services/frontend`'s
  `react-*.md` rules) are mirrored as root-level `.cursor/rules/{nest,react}-*.mdc` files with
  `globs` scoped to the relevant service path(s), since Cursor doesn't look up rules per-service
  the way Claude Code does.

## Planning gap audit

The shared [planning-gap-audit skill](../../.claude/skills/planning-gap-audit/SKILL.md) works in both
Claude Code and Cursor. Use it before recommending the next story, approving a story
specification, starting implementation, changing story order, or marking a story complete.

Examples:

    /planning-gap-audit
    /planning-gap-audit Check whether Story 1.4 is ready for implementation.
    /planning-gap-audit Check whether Story 1.3 can be marked done.

It checks the PRD, architecture, UX, epics, stories, dependencies, Git/PR delivery,
authentication, authorization, cross-service contracts, and testing. It distinguishes BMAD
status, implementation delivery, and end-to-end verification, then reports gaps and remediation
recommendations. The audit is read-only by default and must not modify stories, statuses, or
planning artifacts without explicit approval.
